## A B S T R A C T
Copper-MOFs (Cu-MOFs) have been reported to demonstrate great potential as cardiovascular biomaterials, due to enhanced catalytic ability of ${\mathsf{C}}{\mathsf{u}}^{2+}$ to generate nitric oxide (NO) from endogenous S-nitrosothiols (RSNOs). However, free Cu-MOFs usually show rapid degradation under physiological conditions, resulting in short cat­ alytic half-life and risk of copper ion toxicity. Therefore, how to increase the stability of Cu-MOFs is of great importance in cardiovascular biomaterials research. Herein, we chose M199 MOF as an example and developed Cu-MOF-based scaffold, using the electrospinning method to embed Cu-MOF nanoparticles into polycaprolactone (PCL) fibers. Entrapment of Cu-MOF nanoparticles within PCL could simultaneously enhance Cu-MOF stability in serum and allow for long-term NO catalytic activity, as assessed by in vitro assays and using in situ implantation models. Additionally, the optimized concentration of Cu-MOFs loaded within the scaffolds significantly pro­ moted endothelial cell (EC) migration and increased acetylated low-density lipoprotein (Ac-LDL) uptake. Moreover, Cu-MOF-based scaffolds dramatically inhibited platelet adhesion and activation, which markedly reduced acute thrombosis in arterio-venous shunt models. In situ implantation experiments revealed that the PCL/Cu-MOF scaffolds accelerated the formation of an intact endothelial monolayer. Together, these results suggest that the incorporation of $\mathtt{C u}$ -MOFs into electrospun fibers could serve as a promising approach to achieve stable catalytic performance and long-term activity required for implant materials.
## 1. Introduction
Over the past decade, research on metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) has continued to expand in different fields, such as gas adsorption/ storage, catalysis, sensing, and biomedicine, because of their unique, tunable features and chemical/physical properties[1–3]. Of note, with the fast development of synthetic methods and surface modification strategies, intelligent MOF-based nanoparticles with advanced bioac­ tivity have been designed and developed for biomedical applications[4], including drugs delivery for cancer therapy[5], bio-imaging[6], anti­ microbial[7], bio-sensing[8], and bio-catalysis[9]. Therein, copperbased MOFs (Cu-MOFs) are composed of organic bridging ligands and copper ions, which show high surface area and porosity with enhanced catalysis and longer half-life, compared with other catalysts[10].
Intriguingly, Cu-MOFs have shown great potentials as nitric oxide (NO) catalysts, which have been used in the development of NO-releasing materials with prolonged release duration[11–12]. Recent researches reported that surface immobilization of Cu-MOFs on cardiovascular stents realized controllable in situ NO catalytic generation[13–14]. However, Cu-MOFs tend to be non-stable in physiological and proteincontaining solutions [15], which causes copper ions to burst release and thus renders its catalytic ability difficult to maintain. As such, there is an urgent need to develop a new material technology that can increase the catalytic stability of Cu-MOFs.
Electrospinning is a versatile technique for processing polymers into fibrous materials with diameters ranging from the micrometer to nanometer scale. It readily allows for the incorporation of metals, metal oxides, carbons, and inorganic/organic composites, according to facile tunable parameters during the spinning process. Due to their ease of tunability, electrospun scaffolds have proven to be an attractive prospect for biological, catalytic, improved energy generation, and for environ­ mental and mechanical enhancement of tissues [16]. For example, a variety of electrospinning materials composited with copper particles, such as CuO and $\mathrm{CuSO_{4}}$ , have been explored to be applied as antibac­ terial materials [17], wound healing [18] and bone tissue engineering [19]. Electrospinning has also served as a new method for the loading of nanoparticles into polymer fibers, enabling rapid lyophilization at room temperature for long-term nanoparticle storage[20]. The versatility of this approach has also shown enhanced activity of stabilized nano­ particles, for example, zeolitic imidazolate framework (ZIF)-derived $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles incorporated into porous $N\mathrm{.}$ -doped carbon and exhibited improved redox activity[21]. Electrospinning composite MOF particles have been widely investigated as energy materials with enhanced performance. For example, the porous structure of electro­ spinning can improve the redox electrocatalytic characteristics of ZnCo [22]. In addition, $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ nanoplates assembled on electrospun polyacrylonitrile-metal organic framework-derived carbon fibers were developed for lithium storage[23]. Meanwhile, electrospinning com­ posite MOF, such as Ag MOF and Zn MOF, was proved with superior characterization in the biomedical application[24]. Despite the promise of electrospinning for enhancement of stabilization, the application of electrospinning for MOF stabilization in translational medicine research still remains a relatively unexplored research field.
Herein, we developed a Cu-MOF-based scaffold using the electro­ spinning method to embed Cu-MOF nanoparticles into polycaprolactone (PCL) fibers, which stabilized the Cu-MOFs and enabled long-term NO catalysis and release (Fig. 1), and this Cu-MOF/PCL electrospinning hybrid material could be developed into small diameter vascular grafts (SDVGs) which has enhanced ability to support vascular tissue regen­ eration. The characterization of Cu-MOF nanoparticles and the Cu-MOFbased scaffolds were thoroughly examined by transmission electron microscopy (TEM), X-ray diffraction (XRD) and scanning electron mi­ croscopy (SEM). The catalytic activities of PCL/Cu-MOF scaffolds to generate NO from S-nitrosothiol (RSNO) donors or fresh serum were assessed by Griess assays or nitrate reductase method. The stabilization of Cu-MOFs by incorporation into the PCL scaffold was tested by copper ion release, as measured by inductively coupled plasma mass spec­ trometry (ICP-MS). Long-term controllable NO catalytic ability of CuMOF-based scaffolds was examined using in vitro assays and in situ im­ plantation model. Furthermore, the biological effects of Cu-MOF-based scaffolds on endothelial cell function were also examined. The antithrombotic activity was checked by arteriovenous (AV)-shunt experi­ ment. Finally, the performance of Cu-MOF-based scaffolds as SDVGs was assessed using rat abdominal artery replacement model.
## 2. Materials and methods
## 2.1. Materials
PCL polymer (average Mn, 80 kDa) was purchased from Sigma (St. Louis, MO, USA). Methanol, chloroform and alcohol were purchased from Tianjin Chemical Reagent Company (Tianjin, China). The Griess reaction assay kit was purchased from Nanjing Jiancheng Bioengi­ neering Institute (Nanjing, China). Platelet rich plasma (PRP) was pur­ chased from the Tianjin Blood Center (Tianjin, China). The human sPSelectin enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA) kit was purchased from Multi Sciences Biotech (Zhejiang, China). $^{1,1^{\prime}}$ -dio­ ctadecyl-1–3,3,3′, $3^{\prime}$ -tetramethyl-indo-carbocyanine perchlorate labeled acetylated low density lipoprotein (DiI-Ac-LDL) was purchased from Solarbio (Beijing, China). The lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) Cytotoxicity Assay Kit was purchased from Beyotime Biotechnology (Shanghai, China). All animal experiments were approved by the Animal Experi­ ments Ethical and Welfare of Institute of Radiation Medicine, Chinese Academy of Medical Sciences (IRM-DWLL-2019031).
## 2.2. Preparation of MOF-199 nanoparticle
$63~\mathrm{mg}$ of benzene-1,3,5-tricarboxylate (btc) and $0.125{\mathrm{~mL}}$ of trie­ thylamine were respectively added in $3~\mathrm{mL}$ of deionized water under room temperature to yield $0.1\mathrm{~M~}$ btc triethylammonium salt aqueous solution. $3~\mathrm{mL}$ of $0.1\mathrm{~M~}$ benzene-1, 3, 5-tricarboxylate (btc) triethy­ lammonium salt aqueous solution was added to $300~\mathrm{{mL}}$ of 1:1 (v/v) mixture of ethanol and deionized water to produce solution A. Subse­ quently, $4.5~\mathrm{mL}$ of 0.1 M $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ aqueous solution was added drop­ wise to solution A. The reaction mixture was stirred vigorously for 30 mins at room temperature. The product was washed with ethanol for 3 times to eliminate unreacted reagents. The final purified products were stored in anhydrous ethanol at room temperature.
## 2.3. PCL/Cu-MOF membranes and vascular scaffolds preparation
Membranes and vascular scaffolds were prepared by electrospinning $10\%$ (w/v) PCL and MOF nanoparticle solution. First, PCL pellets were dissolved in chloroform and methanol $\scriptstyle(5:1\ \mathbf{v}/\mathbf{v})$ and stirred at room temperature for $6\mathrm{h}$ . Next, $200~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of methanol was added to suspend the MOF nanoparticles. After ultrasonic dispersion, MOF suspension was added to PCL solution and stirred for $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ . The ratios of MOF nano­ particles to PCL were $0.125\%$ , $0.25\%$ , $0.5\%$ , $1.25\%$ and $2.5\%$ . The fabricated scaffolds were named PCL- $0.125\%$ MOF, PCL- $0.25\%$ MOF, PCL- $0.5\%$ MOF, PCL- $1.25\%$ MOF and PCL- $2.5\%$ MOF. Where PCL-MOF is used, it refers to all scaffolds loaded with MOF nanoparticles. Finally, the prepared solutions were loaded into a $10\mathrm{mL}$ glass syringe with a 21- gauge needle and injected using an automatic pump. The flow rate was set to $2\mathrm{mL}/\mathrm{h}$ and a voltage of $21\mathrm{kV}$ was applied to the solution. Fibers were collected on a rotating mandrel for fibrous membranes and a rotating stainless-steel mandrel (diameter $2\mathrm{mm}$ ) for vascular scaffolds. The rotating speeds were $400~\mathrm{rpm}$ . These scaffolds were vacuum dried for at least $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ to remove residual solvents.
## 2.4. Characterization of $\boldsymbol{C u}$ -MOF nanoparticles and PCL-MOF scaffolds
The particle size of Cu-MOF nanoparticles was determined by Dy­ namic Light Scattering (DLS) and SEM (Thermo Fisher Scientific) was used to observe the morphology of vascular scaffolds and membranes. TEM (Hitachi HT7700) with an accelerating voltage of $120\mathrm{kV}$ was used for imaging. Longitudinal mechanical properties were determined using a tensile-testing machine with a load capacity of $100~\mathrm{N}$ (Instron-3345, USA) at room temperature. Scaffolds (total length, $3\mathrm{cm}$ ; gauge length, 1 cm) were pulled longitudinally at a strain rate of $10\ \mathrm{mm/min}$ until rupture. Tensile strength and ultimate elongation at break were measured. Young’s modulus was calculated from the linear region (up to $5\%$ strain) of the stress–strain curve. These mechanical tests were per­ formed in quadruplicate. XRD was performed using SmartLab 9 kW (Rigaku, Japan) with Cu-Kα radiation $(\lambda=1.54\AA\AA)$ in the intervals $5^{\circ}\leq$ $2\boldsymbol{\Theta}\le3\boldsymbol{0}^{^{\circ}}$ .
## 2.5. Copper ion release measurement
The release of copper ions from PCL-MOF in $0.01\mathrm{M}$ phosphate buffer saline (PBS, $\mathrm{pH}~7.4$ , $37~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ or $10\%$ fetal bovine serum (FBS) was assessed. Briefly, $40\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{PCL}{\cdot}0.125\%$ MOF vascular scaffolds or $0.05\mathrm{mg}$ MOF nanoparticles were added into $2~\mathrm{mL}$ of 0.01 M PBS or $10\%$ FBS solutions and continuously shaken with a speed of $100\mathrm{rpm}$ at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ . At predetermined intervals, $1~\mathrm{mL}$ of release media was taken out for con­ tent measurement and replenished with an equal volume of fresh media at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The amount of the released copper ions was measured by ICPMS.
## 2.6. Cell viability assay
Preparation of scaffold extractions was as follows: $100~\mathrm{{mg}}$ scaffolds with different ratios of MOF nanoparticles (PCL, PCL- $0.125\%$ MOF, PCL$0.25\%$ MOF, PCL- $0.5\%$ MOF, PCL- $\cdot1.25\%$ MOF and PCL- $2.5\%$ MOF) were immersed into $1\ \mathrm{mL}$ of Endothelial Cell Medium (ECM) or $1\ \mathrm{mL}$ of Dulbecco’s Modified Eagle’s medium (DMEM) at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . The extractions of the scaffolds were stored at $4~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ for subsequent experi­ ments. Human Umbilical Vein Endothelial Cells (HUVECs) and Human Smooth Muscle Cells (SMCs) were seeded in 96-well plates $(5\times10^{3}$ cells/well) were incubated for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ and then extractions made above were added incubation for another $24\mathrm{h}$ from the different scaffolds. Cell counting kit-8 (CCK-8; Beyotime, Haimen, China) dilutions containing $20\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of CCK-8 and $200\upmu\mathrm{L}$ ECM was added to each well incubated for an additional $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ . Subsequently, absorbance at $450\mathrm{nm}$ was measured using a microplate reader. Cell viability was expressed as a percentage of the absorbance to that of the control experiment without treatment.
## 2.7. Cell proliferation
To investigate the effect of NO production on HUVECs and SMCs growth, MTT assay was performed. In details, the scaffolds $(\mathbf{n}=5)$ ) of PCL and PCL- $.0.125\%$ MOF were cut into circular discs (diameter $10\mathrm{mm}\mathrm{\dot{\Omega}}$ ) and then placed into 48-well tissue culture plates, which were sterilized overnight by ultraviolet radiation. Before cell seeding, the wells were washed once with PBS. Next, cells at a density of $5\times10^{3}$ in $300~\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ medium were seeded onto the surface of PCL and PCL- $.0.125\%$ MOF membranes. Cells were cultured with medium containing NO donor, $100~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ S-Nitrosoglutathione (GSNO) and $100~\upmu\mathbf{M}$ L-Glutathione (GSH) for 1, 3 and 5 days. The medium was changed with fresh medium con­ taining NO donor every two days. The cells seeded on the tissue culture polystyrene plate (TCP) without scaffolds were set as blank control. At the determined time, MTT assay was carried out. In detail, MTT solution $\mathrm{(5~mg/mL)}$ was added into each well and incubated for $^{4\mathrm{~h~}}$ at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ Medium was then removed and $300~\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ of dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO) was added to dissolve crystals formed by living cells. $100~\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ of the su­ pernatant was transferred to a 96-well plate and read under microplate reader. The absorbance at $490\mathrm{nm}$ was measured. The OD490 of that on TCP with medium without cells was set as negative control. Cell viability was expressed as a percentage of the absorbance to that of the blank control at the first day without NO donor treatment.
## 2.8. In vitro catalytic NO generation
GSNO, one of the most typical endogenous RSNO species used for NO release tests, was synthesized according to previous report[25]. In brief, $10~\mathrm{mg}$ of scaffold was placed into a $15~\mathrm{mL}$ centrifuge tube, with un­ modified PCL used as a control ${\bf(n=3)}$ ). The composition of the final working solution was $100~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ GSNO and $100~\upmu\mathbf{M}$ GSH. A working so­ lution of $10\mathrm{mL}$ was added into each centrifuge tube and incubated for 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, and $^{6\mathrm{~h~}}$ at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , under darkness and with gently shaking. Accumulated NO was measured using Griess assay [26] at the indicated time points. An aliquot of $50\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of supernatant was incubated with $50\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of Griess reagent I, and this was followed by the addition of $50~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of Griess reagent II. The concentration of NO was acquired via a standard curve through a series gradient of sodium nitrite $\left(\mathrm{NaNO}_{2}\right)$ . To further investigate catalytic recyclability of PCL-MOF scaffolds, in vitro catalytic NO generation was carried out for a minimum of 3 times, and the vol­ ume of working solution taken was replaced each time.
## 2.9. NO generation in serum
To simulate the catalytic environment within peripheral blood, scaffolds (PCL and PCL- $0.125\%$ MOF) were immersed into fresh serum and incubated for 1, 2, 3, and $^{4\mathrm{~h~}}$ at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ away from light and under gentle shaking. At the indicated time points, NO generation was measured using serum NO assay kits (Jiancheng, China, A012) using the nitrate reductase method, according to manufacturer’s protocol [26]. Absorbance was measured at $550\mathrm{nm}$ using a microplate reader. Distilled water was as a blank, and the $100\upmu\mathrm{M}$ sodium nitrite as a standard. The final concentration of NO was calculated according to the formula (1):
(1) NO concentration $(\upmu\mathbf{M})=$ (sample OD-blank OD)/(standard ODblank ${\mathrm{OD}})\times100\ {\upmu\mathrm{M}}$
## 2.10. Ac-LDL uptake assays
To observe the effects of NO generation on Ac-LDL uptake by HUVECs, cells were seeded into a 48-well plate $(2.0\times10^{4}$ cell / well) and incubated for $24\mathrm{h}$ . Then, DiI-Ac-LDL $(20\upmu\mathrm{g/mL})$ was added to each well and incubated at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ . The cells were washed 5 times with PBS and fixed in $4\%$ paraformaldehyde. The nuclei were then counter­ stained with $4^{\prime}$ , 6-diamidino-2-phenylindole (DAPI), and the samples were viewed using a confocal laser scanning microscope (CLSM, Zeiss. LSM710, Germany).
## 2.11. HUVECs migration assay
HUVECs were labeled with CytoTrace Orange CMTMR (Cayman Chemical, Ann Arbor, MI, USA) and then seeded onto PCL and PCL-MOF membranes for evaluation of cell migration. Live cell imaging was per­ formed under a Live Cell Imaging System (Deltavision ultra). The cells were cultured at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $5\%\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ throughout the monitoring process. Images were captured every 5 min over a course of $^{8\mathrm{h}}$ . Each individual cell was tracked using the “Manual Tracking Plug-in” of the NIH ImageJ software to output $(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})$ positions. The cell migration rate was calcu­ lated from the formula (2),
(2) $\mathbf{v}={\mathbf{}}{\mathbf{}}\mathbf{S}/\mathbf{t}$ $\mathrm{\Deltat}=480~\mathrm{min}^{\cdot}$ ).
The Euclidean distance was acquired by Chemotaxis and Migration Tool 2.0 (IBIDI, Germany) every $5\ \mathrm{min}$ . Using comparisons of the Euclidian distance with the accumulated distance, the directness was acquired and represented as analysis of the directness of cell trajectories. 30 cells from each group were tracked for the calculation of cell migration and trajectory.
## 2.12. In vitro hemocompatibility test
The samples (diameter $1\ \mathrm{cm})$ were placed in 48-well cell culture plates ${\bf(n=4)}$ ) and incubated with $400~\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ PRP at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ under static conditions. After incubation, non-adhering platelets and proteins were removed by rinsing with PBS. Then, $2.5\%$ glutaraldehyde solution was used to fix the samples for $^{\textrm{1h}}$ at $4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . After washing with PBS three times, the samples were dehydrated using an ascending series of ethanol from $70\%$ to $100\%$ , each for $15\mathrm{min}$ . After thoroughly dried, the samples were observed using SEM. Platelet staining and fluorescence microscopy were carried out as described previously[27]. In brief, activated plate­ lets were stained with anti-CD62p (P-Selectin) antibody (Sigma) for 1 h at $37^{\circ}\mathbf{C}.$ . The number of adherent platelets was acquired by detecting the amount of LDH after cell lysis. The adherent platelets were rinsed with PBS three times and samples were permeated with $0.05\%$ TritonX-100. An LDH Cytotoxicity Assay Kit was used to measure the LDH activity in the lysed platelet suspensions. The absorbance was measured at $490\mathrm{nm}$ using a microplate reader (iMark, Bio-Rad, USA). A human sP-Selectin ELISA Kit was used to measure the amounts of activated platelets ac­ cording the manufacturer’s instructions, and the absorbance was measured at $450\ \mathrm{nm}$ using a microplate reader.
## 2.13. Ex vivo evaluation of thrombogenicity
Six male Sprague Dawley rats were used for ex vivo evaluation of thrombogenicity. Anti-thrombotic properties of tubular scaffolds were evaluated in the AV-shunt experiment under general anesthesia. The abdominal aorta and interior vena cava were isolated, and then the extracorporeal circuit (ECC) was connected to the circulation using indwelling needles. The experiment was carried out avoiding light. The circulation was kept open for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ , and then the scaffolds were removed from the animal and rinsed with PBS. The residual clots on the surface of the scaffolds were collected, weighed and photographed. The specimen was taken out and fixed in $2.5\%$ glutaraldehyde solution overnight. Samples were observed by SEM after dehydration.
## 2.14. In situ implantation
In total, 24 Sprague Dawley rats (body weight: $250\mathrm{-}300\mathrm{~g~}$ , male) were anesthetized, and the PCL scaffolds and PCL- $0.125\%$ MOF scaffolds were grafted into rat abdominal arteries separately by vascular anasto­ mosis, as previously described[26]. In brief, the animals were anes­ thetized with $10\%$ chloral hydrate at a dose of $330\mathrm{mg/kg}$ . Heparin (100 units $/\mathbf{kg})$ was injected through the tail intravenous injection for anti­ coagulation. The abdominal fur was removed, disinfected with $75\%$ ethanol and iodine. A midline laparotomy incision was made and the abdominal aorta was isolated, clamped, and transected. A vascular graft (diameter $2.0\mathrm{mm}$ , length $1.0\mathrm{cm}\dot{}$ was used as an artery replacement. It was sutured to the native artery with 9–0 suture by discontinuous endto-end anastomosis. Finally, the skin was closed with 3–0 suture. The animals did not return to their cages until they woke up. No anticoag­ ulants were used postoperatively. The patency of the scaffolds was examined at 2, 4, and 12 weeks after implantation. The rats were sacrificed by injection of an overdose of chloral hydrate, and scaffolds were cut into two pieces from the middle. One piece was for frozen crosssection and the other was longitudinally cut into three pieces. One of these pieces was used for SEM imaging and en-face staining, the other was frozen in optimal cutting temperature compound (OCT) for longitude-sectioning and CD31 staining.
## 2.15. Immunofluorescence staining
The frozen sections were fixed using cold acetone for $10\ \mathrm{min}$ , airdried and rinsed with $0.01\ \mathrm{mM}$ PBS. The slides were blocked using $5\%$ normal goat serum, followed by incubation with primary antibodies, mouse anti-CD31 (CD31,1:100, Abcam, USA), mouse anti- $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{\vec{a}}_{} $ -smooth muscle actin ( $\mathrm{\dot{\Omega}}({\bf{\Omega}})$ -SMA; 1:200, Boster, China), or mouse anti-smooth muscle myosin heavy chain I (MYH, 1:200, Abcam, UK), overnight at $4^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ . Then the slides were rinsed with PBS for six times, and Alexa Fluor 488 goat anti-mouse IgG (1:200, Invitrogen, Thermo Fisher Scientific) was applied for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ at room temperature. The nuclei were counterstained with DAPI containing mounting solution (DAPi-Fluoromount-G, Thermo Fisher Scientific). Slides were observed under a fluorescence microscope (Zeiss, Germany).
## 2.16. Histological staining
The cross sections of explanted scaffolds ( ${\mathrm{~(6~\upmum}}$ in thickness) were stained with hematoxylin and eosin (H&E). The images were photo­ graphed by an upright microscope (Olympus, USA).
## 2.17. En-face immunofluorescence staining
The explanted scaffolds were blocked with $2\%$ goat serum albumin for $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ at room temperature. Then, the samples were incubated with primary antibody against endothelial cells marker CD31 overnight at $4~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}.$ . After washing six times with PBS, Alexa-Fluor 488 labeled sec­ ondary antibodies were used to incubate the samples. The morphology of endothelial cell distribution on the explanted scaffolds was imaged under a Laser Scanning Confocal Microscope (LSCM).
