## 3. Results
## 3.1. Physical characterization of $\boldsymbol{C u}$ -MOF nanoparticles and PCL-MOF fiber scaffolds
The Cu-MOF nanoparticles were synthesized and then the crystals were observed under TEM. As shown in Fig. 1a, The Cu-MOF nano­ particles were square with the side length approximately $190~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . To check the dispersion of the Cu-MOF nanoparticles in the solution, DLS was tested and results showed that the average of nanoparticle size and polydispersity index (PDI) were $438.3\ \mathrm{nm}$ and 0.166, respectively (Fig. 1a), which suggested that some of the nanoparticles were aggre­ gated. Then PCL-MOF scaffolds were prepared by electrospinning the PCL solution with various concentrations of MOF nanoparticles. From the macroscopic view, the PCL-MOF scaffold displayed light blue due to the existence of Cu-MOF nanoparticles (Fig. 1b). Subsequently, XRD were performed and results showed that the major diffraction peaks associated with MOF nanoparticles at 9.5, 12, $19.3^{\circ}$ and PCL at 21.6 and $23.95^{\circ}$ were observed in the diffraction pattern of the PCL-MOF scaf­ folds, which confirm the MOF particles existence in the PCL-MOF scaf­ fold (Fig. 1c). Furthermore, SEM was performed to check the fibers morphology of PCL-MOF, and the images showed no significant differ­ ence between fibers in the PCL-MOF scaffold (Fig. 1d) and PCL scaffold (Figure S1). Moreover, the distribution of MOF crystals within fibers was studied using TEM. As shown in Fig. 1e, the MOF nanoparticles were mostly randomly dispersed within the PCL-MOF fibers, as well as a few were on the surface of the fibers.
## 3.2. In vitro catalytic property of PCL-MOF fiber scaffolds
In vitro catalytic generation of NO by PCL-MOF scaffolds was firstly assessed in PBS with RSNO donor. As shown in Fig. 1g, NO releasing could be detected in all the scaffolds containing different ratios of MOF nanoparticles. As increasing the ratios of MOF nanoparticles from $0.125\%$ to $1.25\%$ , NO release increased from $50~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ to $150~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ over $^{6\mathrm{{h}}}$ (Fig. 1g-h), which indicated that NO generation catalyzed by PCL-MOF scaffolds was dose-dependent on the load of Cu-MOF nanoparticles. After replacement of GSNO, all PCL-MOF substrates exhibited similar catalytic behavior as the initial test, which proved the catalytic behavior of Cu-MOF in the scaffolds could be repeated use, indicating that the scaffolds possessed the ability to produce a stable and controllable generation of NO in a long-term (Fig. 1h). To further verify the catalytic behavior of Cu-MOF scaffolds under the physiological conditions, in vitro catalytic generation of NO was carried out in fresh serum. PCL- $.0.125\%$ MOF was chosen as a representative, and data showed that PCL-MOF scaffold exhibited a strong catalytic capability to accelerate NO gener­ ation compared to that of PCL scaffold (Fig. 1f).
## 3.3. The stability of Cu-MOF nanoparticles in the PCL-MOF fiber scaffolds
Furthermore, the stability of copper ions within the membranes was examined. As shown Figure S2, the copper ions released $24.94\%$ in PBS and $68.81\%$ in $10\%$ FBS solution of bare Cu-MOF nanoparticles within $48\mathrm{h}$ , while the Cu-MOF nanoparticles present in the fibers released very few in the initial stage in both solutions. And even after 30 days, the PCL$0.125\%$ MOF only released $6.17\%$ and $20.32\%$ of the loaded copper ions in PBS and $10\%$ FBS, respectively (Fig. 1i). These results demonstrated that the fiber scaffolds effectively improved Cu-MOF stability, due to reduced exposure of Cu-MOF to the protein-containing solution.
## 3.4. Screening the optimal loading ratio of Cu-MOF nanoparticles by biocompatibility assay
The cell compatibility is the primary factor for the scaffolds appli­ cation in the biomedical field. As known, high concentration of copper ions could result in cell toxicity. Therefore, the optimal concentration of nanoparticles in PCL-MOF scaffold was screened by the cell viability assay. Herein, cell viability towards HUVECs or SMCs on the PCL-MOF scaffolds with various nanoparticles load ratio were determined via CCK-8 assay. As shown in Figure S3A, the loading concentration of $0.125\%$ did not affect the growth of HUVECs when compared to the PCL group. When the loading concentration of copper nanoparticles increased, the scaffolds exhibited high toxicity to HUVECs. The results were similar to SMCs. In details, SMCs treated with extractions from PCL- $0.125\%$ MOF had slightly increased growth when compared to the PCL group after $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . And if the ratio of PCL and MOF nanoparticles exceeded $0.125\%$ , the resultant extraction markedly inhibited SMC growth due to the high concentration of ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ , and thus copper ion toxicity (Figure S3B). Therefore, a ratio of $0.125\%$ , which showed no toxicity against either HUVECs or SMCs, was chosen for all subsequent experiments.
3.5. The effect of PCL-MOF scaffolds on HUVECs and SMCs function in vitro
The effect of NO generation on endothelial Ac-LDL uptake was evaluated. The DiI-Ac-LDL fluorescence images indicating intracellular Ac-LDL content in HUVECs were shown in Fig. 2a. The amount of AcLDL taken up by HUVECs cultured on the PCL-MOF substrates in the presence of GSNO was higher than the amount endocytosed by the other three groups. In parallel, quantitative analysis of the DiI-Ac-LDL fluo­ rescence intensity confirmed that PCL- $0.125\%$ MOF with GSNO sup­ plement had the most prominent effect on increased Ac-LDL uptake (Fig. 2b).
The proliferation of HUVECs and SMCs cultured on the PCL and PCL$0.125\%$ MOF substrates was evaluated by MTT assay, in the presence or absence of NO donor. As shown in Fig. 2c, there was a significantly greater proliferation of HUVECs when cultured on the PCL- $0.125\%$ MOF substrates, compared to PCL group after 5 days. For SMCs, we observed a substantial inhibitory effect on proliferation when cultured on the PCL$0.125\%$ MOF substrates, whereas a marked increase was observed when SMCs were cultured on PCL substrates (Fig. 2d). These results indicated that the bioavailability and bioactivity of NO generated by MOF catal­ ysis can stimulate HUVECs proliferation, whilst inhibiting SMCs proliferation.
The migration behavior of HUVECs on substrates with different conditions was evaluated and statistically analyzed (Fig. 3a). The HUVECs on the PCL and PCL- $.0.125\%$ MOF membranes without GSNO supplement were inactive, with behavior of random vibration around the initial seeding positions. The HUVECs on the PCL membranes with GSNO added also exhibited no obvious effect on cell migration. In contrast, after incubation for $^{8\mathrm{h}}$ , HUVECs exposed to PCL- $0.125\%$ MOF scaffolds supplemented with GSNO showed the largest effect on migration rate and trajectory. As shown in Fig. 3a-b, the position of cells exhibited changes in the PCL- $0.125\%$ MOF group, more than the other three groups. The cell migration rate and directness of HUVECs were also obviously higher than the other three groups throughout the monitoring time (Fig. 3c-d). These results suggest that the observed increase in cell migration is likely due to NO generation, which may contribute to a rapid endothelialization response.
## 3.6. In vitro hemocompatibility of PCL-MOF scaffolds
To evaluate the NO release effect on platelet adhesion and activation in vitro, PCL and PCL- $0.125\%$ MOF membranes were incubated with PRP for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ . $\mathrm{CD}62\mathrm{p}$ staining showed that a large amount of platelets were activated and adhered onto the sample PCL, PCL- $0.125\%$ MOF, and PCL without NO donor supplement. In comparison, the number of activated platelets on the PCL- $.0.125\%$ MOF substrates with donor supplemented was significantly lower than in the other three groups. As shown in Fig. $4\mathrm{a}$ , the substrates without donor led to high amounts of platelets and plasma proteins adhering onto the fiber surfaces. For the PCL mem­ branes with donor supplement, there was a slight reduction in numbers of platelets and plasma protein absorbed on the membranes. Further­ more, PCL- $0.125\%$ MOF substrates with GSNO donor exhibited an obvious inhibition in platelet adherence and fibrin formation, compared with another three groups. Determination of the adherent platelet was further performed with LDH measurement of lysed platelets (Fig. 4c). The LDH results also indicated that the platelet adhesion levels were largely inhibited on the PCL- $0.125\%$ MOF samples after GSNO was added (Fig. 4b). In accordance with CD62p staining, P-Selectin expres­ sion was also dramatically lower than the other groups (Fig. 4d). These results confirm that PCL- $0.125\%$ MOF substrates could effectively inhibit platelet adhesion and activation.
## 3.7. The mechanical properties of PCL-0.125% MOF scaffold
The uniaxial tensile test was carried out to check the mechanical properties of PCL- $0.125\%$ MOF whether strong enough as small diam­ eter vascular graft. The stress–strain curves of the scaffolds were shown in Figure S4. The elastic modulus was about $12.85\mathrm{MPa}$ for PCL scaffolds and $11.52\mathrm{MPa}$ for PCL- $.0.125\%$ MOF group. The maximum stress of PCL was $7.10\ensuremath{\mathrm{MPa}}$ and 6.25 MPa for PCL- $0.125\%$ MOF group. The maximum strain was $55.16\%$ for PCL and $54.29\%$ for PCL- $0.125\%$ MOF group. No significant differences of the mechanical properties existed between the two groups. By combination of our previous study related to PCL scaffolds as small diameter vascular graft [26], it infers that PCL-MOF scaffold possessed the mechanical strength capable for small diameter vascular grafts.
3.8. Ex-vivo catalysis, NO generation and anti-adhesion of platelets capability of PCL-MOF scaffolds post-implantation
An AV-shunt experiment was performed to obtain further insight into the anti-thrombogenic properties of PCL- $0.125\%$ MOF under the arterial blood flow in rats for $^\textrm{\scriptsize2h}$ implant time. Stereomicroscopy images showed that the lumens of MOF scaffolds were clean and displayed no sign of clot formation. In contrast, the lumen of PCL scaffolds became red and the thickness of the wall increased (Fig. 5a). The clots on the surface of vascular scaffolds were collected and weighed. The quanti­ tative analysis of the thrombus weight was significantly reduced on the PCL- $0.125\%$ MOF scaffolds compared to PCL scaffolds ( $\cdot13.8\mathrm{\mg}$ and 5 mg, respectively) (Fig. 5c). To further understand the influence of PCL$0.125\%$ MOF scaffolds on platelet adhesion, the surface morphology of the scaffolds was analyzed using SEM. The results indicated that plate­ lets and formation of fibrin were distributed on the lumen of PCL scaf­ folds. In contrast, for PCL- $0.125\%$ MOF scaffolds, there were few platelets observable on the luminal surface. Quantification of platelets adhesion exhibited that PCL scaffolds had a considerably higher per­ centage of adhered platelets after $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ of implant and circulation time (Fig. 5d).
## 3.9. Performance of the MOF scaffolds as a rat abdominal artery substitute
In situ performance of PCL- $0.125\%$ MOF scaffolds as vascular scaf­ folds was evaluated (Fig. 6). The implants were harvested at 2, 4 and 12 weeks. One scaffold was occluded in the PCL group at the 2 and 12 week time points. All scaffolds were patent at each examined time point with no macroscopic thrombus formations. The luminal surfaces of the explanted scaffolds were analyzed by SEM to observe the endothelial cell coverage at three different positions: near sutured sites, at the midportion and at the site between them. The PCL group was rarely covered by endothelial cells, and large fibers were still visible at week 2 (Figure S5). There was amounts of platelets adhered onto the luminal surface of the PCL group. In contrast, PCL- $0.125\%$ MOF scaffolds were almost completely covered with ECs, except the mid-portion at 2 weeks. In addition, the immunofluorescence staining, and its corresponding quantitative data also exhibited a higher degree of endothelial cell coverage in the Cu-MOF-based scaffolds, when compared with PCL groups (Figure S6 and Fig. 6i). After 4 weeks post-implantation, scaf­ folds loaded with MOF were fully covered by a confluent layer of endothelial cells (Fig. 6b). The statistical analysis of the endothelial cell coverage area revealed that PCL- $0.125\%$ MOF group had much higher endothelialization rate compared to the PCL group $(82.46\%$ and $56.72\%$ , respectively) (Fig. 6h). En-face staining showed that the lumen surfaces in PCL- $0.125\%$ MOF groups were completely covered by cobblestone-like $\mathrm{CD}31^{+}$ cells, orientated in the direction of blood flow, indicating resemblance to native endothelium (Fig. 6d-e). The ratio of the endothelial cell long axis to short axis on the PCL- $0.125\%$ MOF group was about three-fold that of the ratio in PCL groups (Fig. 6c), also indicating that endothelial cells were distributed on the lumen along the direction of the blood flow. Consistent with the result of SEM and en-face staining, the immunofluorescence staining on the longitudinal sections also exhibited a monolayer that was completely formed (Fig. 6f-g). Moreover, higher endothelial cell coverage, as calculated by immuno­ fluorescence staining, was also seen in the PCL- $.0.125\%$ MOF group at 4 weeks (Fig. 6i). After 12 weeks, all the scaffolds were completely covered with endothelial cells, as shown by SEM and immunofluores­ cence staining (Figure S7-8). These results suggested that NO generation induced by PCL- $0.125\%$ MOF scaffolds facilitated rapid endothelialization.
Furthermore, our results showed that the majority of SMCs around the PCL scaffolds were the $\upalpha$ -SMA-positive, synthetic phenotype. In comparison, a thinner neointimal was seen in the PCL- $0.125\%$ MOF scaffolds than that in the PCL group (Figure S9-10). Moreover, after implantation for 2, 4 and 12 weeks, NO generation rate of PCL- $.0.125\%$ MOF scaffolds remained as high as before implantation (Fig. 6g). After replenishing GSNO donor, all scaffolds loaded with MOF still had the capability to catalyze NO generation (Fig. 6h-i). These data indicate that the fabricated scaffolds exhibit a long-lasting stability of NO catalytic activity.
## 4. Discussion
Cu-MOFs were reported to show great potential as cardiovascular biomaterials due to their catalytic ability to generate NO from endoge­ nous RSNOs. However, the instability of Cu-MOFs under physiological conditions leads to their shorter catalytic half-life and increased risk of copper ion toxicity[12]. In this investigation, we prepared $\mathtt{C u}$ -MOFbased scaffolds using the electrospinning method to embed Cu-MOF nanoparticles into PCL fibers. We showed that PCL/Cu-MOF scaffolds as small diameter vascular scaffolds preserved Cu-MOF stability and long-term NO catalytic activity, promoted EC migration and increased Ac-LDL uptake. Moreover, Cu-MOF-based scaffolds dramatically inhibited platelet adhesion and activation, and attenuated acute thrombosis in AV-shunt model. In situ implantation experiments revealed that PCL-MOF scaffolds accelerated the formation of an intact endothelial monolayer formation. Taken together, we provided a method to improve Cu-MOF catalytic stability under physiological conditions, and its applications in regenerative medicine.
The stability is the primary requirement for MOFs’ application in the biomedical field. It is determined by the properties of both metal ions and organic linkers, including the physicochemical properties of metal cations, such as charge, size, electronegativity, as well as length and rigidity of inorganic linkers[28]. The strength of coordination bond and covalent bond between them determines the stability of MOF nano­ particles. External stimuli, such as chemical, heat, light, radiation, electronic and mechanical factors, will weaken the coordination bonds inside MOFs and eventually lead to the destruction of MOF structure. As for the instability of MOFs in the physiological environment are usually caused by chemical destruction, coordination competition between organic ligands and coordination substances such as water molecules, hydroxide ions, phosphate buffer and bovine serum albumin, with central metal ions[29]. Altogether, avoiding the contacting between MOFs and physiological solution environment is beneficial for
maintaining its stability.
One importance of the present work is that we provided a facile method by using of electrospinning technology to solve the instability issue of Cu-MOF nanoparticles when contacting protein solution. As known, the application of Cu-MOFs for biomedical purposes so far has been restricted and mainly focused on the immobilization of MOFs onto the surface of substrates, using methodologies including direct growing of crystals[30], dip-coating[31], electrochemistry[32], hydrothermal [33] and layer-by-layer self-assembly[34]. The issues that exist in these approaches are predominantly caused by the weak interactions between MOFs and the chosen substrates, which inevitably lead to the loss of function within a very short time period. As reported previously, Hatton et. al., grew MOF crystals directly on electrospun scaffolds, the immo­ bilization was instable and MOFs easily detached from the scaffold surfaces[35]. The strategy of the present work was to embed Cu-MOFs into electrospun scaffolds (Fig. 1). In this way, it effectively avoids the direct exposure of Cu-MOFs to serum and significantly reduces the leach of copper ions. As seen from the Fig. 1i and Figure S2, the copper ion released around $60\%$ and $20\%$ from the bare Cu-MOF nanoparticles in FBS and PBS solution in the initial $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ , while it was dramatically reduced in the PCL-MOF groups in FBS and PBS solution. As for the reason why the degradation of MOFs in FBS was more comparing to in PBS, we consider it is probably due to the presence of albumin in FBS. Copper ions and albumin tend to form coordination bonds[36,37], which make the stability of coordination bonds inside MOFs worse, and eventually leads to structural destruction. These data demonstrate that embedding Cu-MOFs within PCL would result in the minimal decom­ position of Cu-MOFs and would ensure the prolonged lifetime of the material and improved applicability to long-term biomedical applications.
NO is a versatile gasotransmitter molecule that participates in various physiological processes such as cardiovascular homeostasis[38], immunomodulation[39] and tumor growth[40]. NO-releasing/ generating materials have shown promising prospectus for application as cardiovascular stents and SDVGs by realizing rapid endothelialization and inhibition of restenosis[41–43]. Two main strategies have been put forward, NO-releasing materials and in situ catalytic approaches. NOreleasing materials by conjugation or encapsulation of NO donor have been restricted due to issues such as limited storage and burst release [44,45], whilst NO delivery via catalytic approach has been shown to be capable of continuous and steady NO generation[8,46,47]. Cu-MOFs as a catalyst for generating a continuous amount of NO in situ provides new insights into ways to approach cardiovascular biomaterial fabrication. Herein, we successfully developed a new type of copper ions loaded small diameter vascular scaffold using Cu-MOF nanoparticles instead of conventional copper salts, which showed several advantages. Using the Cu-MOF nanoparticles could reduce the $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ toxicity by decreasing the copper ions burst release compared to inorganic copper ion catalysts. Meanwhile, Cu-MOFs have improved catalytic performance because of their large mesoporous surface area. Additionally, combination of the Cu-MOF nanoparticles and electrospinning technology, help us to overcome the issue that Cu-MOFs tend to decompose when exposed to protein-containing solution[48,49]. As known, the stability of a catalyst to continuously generate NO over a prolonged time period is an important determinant for the performance of implants[50], as NO generation within therapeutic ranges could inhibit acute thrombosis formation during the early implantation phase[51] and limited intimal hyperplasia from appearing during the middle and later stages of im­ plantation. Our fabricated electrospun scaffolds loaded with Cu-MOFs had sustained catalysis even after 12 weeks of in situ implantation, as shown in the Fig. 6 j-k, and PCL/Cu-MOFs maintained a relatively high level of catalytic generation of NO. Compared to direct immobilization of MOFs onto scaffold surface[13,14], the method shown here enabled prolonged NO release due to the favorable and protective encapsulation of Cu-MOFs within PCL fibers.
Furthermore, the enhanced stability of our fabricated scaffolds, which carried the benefits of structural support and copper ion catalysis, enables the use of these MOF-scaffolds in a broad range of applications, including but not limited to: antibacterial materials dressings[52], waste-water treatment meshwork[53], promoter of copper-based click reactions[54], $\ensuremath{\mathbf{O}}_{2}$ release from catalyzed $\mathrm{{H_{2}O_{2}[55]}}$ , and chemical detection test paper[30]. Collectively, this work provides a new sight into the catalytic field and it is expected to extend $\mathtt{C u}$ -MOFs into more fields of research and technology.
## 5. Conclusion
The present work reported a facile method to maintain the long-term catalytic performance of Cu-MOFs through electrospinning technology. The fabrication of anti-thrombus artificial blood vessel through embedded Cu-MOFs within PCL fiber scaffolds led to slow release of copper ions which could facilitate a sustained and controlled NO gen­ eration by long-lasting catalytic process between ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ and NO donor. Importantly, the stable NO generation catalyzed by PCL-MOF scaffolds was demonstrated to accelerate endothelial cell migration in vitro, pro­ moted endothelial monolayer formation in situ, which indicated a strong potential for vascular tissue regeneration. Collectively, our work pro­ vides a new approach to prepare the NO generation biomaterial in combination with the advantages of Cu-MOF nanoparticles and elec­ trospinning method, which showed long-term NO generation ability and great potential application in cardiovascular tissue engineering and
related medical therapies.
